WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
An error that propagates is only useful if the coordinator can act on it.

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:10.439
The propagation contract defines the error envelope structure, how severity levels map

3
00:00:10.439 --> 00:00:16.040
to coordinator policies, how partial success state prevents duplicate work, and why item

4
00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:21.639
potency is non-negotiable when retries are part of your recovery design.

5
00:00:21.639 --> 00:00:23.559
Let's build this out.

6
00:00:23.559 --> 00:00:29.000
When a failure crosses the boundary, it should arrive as a package the coordinator can act on.

7
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:34.400
That package is the error envelope, a structured object that carries failure information from

8
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:37.319
a sub-agent to its coordinator.

9
00:00:37.319 --> 00:00:43.520
Think of it as a formal handoff, with typed fields rather than a free text string.

10
00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:47.279
Severity level is the classification embedded in that envelope.

11
00:00:47.279 --> 00:00:54.439
It can be warning, recoverable, or fatal, and each level maps to a specific coordinator action.

12
00:00:54.439 --> 00:01:00.759
Map mapping is the policy, and it has to be designed ahead of time, not improvised at runtime.

13
00:01:00.759 --> 00:01:06.919
Item potency is the third piece, the guarantee that retrying an operation produces the same

14
00:01:06.919 --> 00:01:09.480
outcome as running it once.

15
00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:17.360
It's what keeps a coordinator retry from causing double writes, duplicate charges, or conflicting state.

16
00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:20.720
Let's look at what a well-designed error envelope contains.

17
00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:26.319
The most important field is error type, a machine-readable category that the coordinator

18
00:01:26.319 --> 00:01:28.599
can branch on programmatically.

19
00:01:28.599 --> 00:01:33.599
This is what allows automated recovery without parsing free text messages.

20
00:01:33.599 --> 00:01:38.559
The second field is severity, which drives the coordinator's response policy.

21
00:01:38.559 --> 00:01:43.919
The third field is context, the sub-agent identifier, the task reference, and enough

22
00:01:43.919 --> 00:01:49.419
of the relevant input state that the coordinator can reconstruct what was happening when the

23
00:01:49.419 --> 00:01:51.059
failure occurred.

24
00:01:51.059 --> 00:01:56.760
Some envelopes also include a recovery hint field, a structured suggestion from the sub-agent

25
00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:59.260
about what the coordinator might try.

26
00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:04.000
The coordinator isn't obligated to follow the hint, but having it available speeds up

27
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:07.900
automated recovery decisions considerably.

28
00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:11.160
Here's why the envelope structure matters so much.

29
00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:16.559
When a coordinator receives a structured error object, it can branch deterministically.

30
00:02:16.559 --> 00:02:21.520
It reads the severity field, looks up the matching policy, and executes the appropriate

31
00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:25.759
response, retry, fallback, or escalate.

32
00:02:25.759 --> 00:02:29.240
That logic is testable, auditable, and fast.

33
00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:34.520
When the coordinator receives an unstructured string, something like operation failed in

34
00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:37.600
step 3, it can't route reliably.

35
00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:41.399
A human has to read it and decide what to do next.

36
00:02:41.399 --> 00:02:45.960
Automated recovery becomes impossible without brittle string matching that breaks the moment

37
00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:48.360
someone changes a log message.

38
00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:53.199
The difference between structured and unstructured error propagation is the difference between

39
00:02:53.199 --> 00:02:59.919
a system that recovers automatically and one that requires human intervention every single time.

40
00:02:59.919 --> 00:03:05.960
The severity levels in the error envelope each map to a specific coordinator behavior, and

41
00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:11.000
that mapping has to be defined in the system design, not discovered at runtime.

42
00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:15.880
A warning level means the coordinator logs the event and continues executing.

43
00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:16.880
Something stops.

44
00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:23.000
A recoverable severity means the coordinator should attempt recovery, typically by retrying

45
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:27.039
with exponential backoff or routing to a fallback agent.

46
00:03:27.039 --> 00:03:30.820
The task may still complete, just via a different path.

47
00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:36.000
A fatal severity means the coordinator halts the dependent task chain.

48
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:41.759
It can't proceed safely, and it escalates to a human operator who can make a judgment call.

49
00:03:41.759 --> 00:03:46.679
The insight here is that severity without a corresponding policy is meaningless.

50
00:03:46.679 --> 00:03:50.919
Labels only matter when they trigger defined behavior.

51
00:03:50.919 --> 00:03:57.199
When the coordinator decides to attempt recovery on a propagated failure, it has four main options.

52
00:03:57.199 --> 00:04:03.600
Exponential backoff means retrying the same sub-agent with increasing wait intervals between attempts.

53
00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:09.160
This works well for transient resource constraints like rate limits or temporary unavailability.

54
00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:14.360
A fallback agent routes the task to an alternate implementation, perhaps a different model

55
00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:19.440
or a rule-based system, when the primary agent is consistently failing.

56
00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:23.920
Partial completion acceptance means the coordinator takes whatever work the sub-agent completed

57
00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:27.940
and adjusts the remaining plan to skip the failed step.

58
00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:33.279
And human escalation pauses the task chain entirely and surfaces the failure for a judgment

59
00:04:33.279 --> 00:04:35.799
call that automated policy can't make.

60
00:04:35.839 --> 00:04:43.079
A well-designed coordinator has all four available and selects based on severity and error type.

61
00:04:43.079 --> 00:04:46.640
Multi-step sub-agent tasks introduce a specific complication.

62
00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:49.600
What happens when the failure occurs partway through?

63
00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:55.660
A sub-agent might complete steps 1 through 3 successfully before failing on step 4.

64
00:04:55.660 --> 00:05:01.100
If the coordinator retries the entire task, steps 1 through 3 execute again, and whatever

65
00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:04.500
side effects they produced are now doubled.

66
00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:08.959
The solution is to include a completed steps field in the error envelope.

67
00:05:08.959 --> 00:05:14.179
This tells the coordinator exactly how far the sub-agent got before failing.

68
00:05:14.179 --> 00:05:19.619
The coordinator can then retry starting from step 4, or route a different agent to pick

69
00:05:19.619 --> 00:05:21.220
up from that point.

70
00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:26.100
Without this field, the coordinator has no way to distinguish failed at the start from

71
00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:29.299
failed after most of the work was already done.

72
00:05:29.299 --> 00:05:32.540
And safe resumption isn't possible.

73
00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:36.339
One more safeguard deserves its own treatment because it's what makes coordinator retries

74
00:05:36.339 --> 00:05:40.739
safe when sub-agents interact with external systems.

75
00:05:40.739 --> 00:05:45.459
Consider a sub-agent that writes a record to a database, sends an API request, or places

76
00:05:45.459 --> 00:05:47.179
a message on a queue.

77
00:05:47.179 --> 00:05:52.779
If the coordinator retries that sub-agent after a propagated failure, without idempotency,

78
00:05:52.779 --> 00:05:55.940
the external write executes a second time.

79
00:05:55.940 --> 00:05:57.260
The record is duplicated.

80
00:05:57.260 --> 00:05:58.540
The charge is doubled.

81
00:05:58.540 --> 00:06:01.079
The message is sent twice.

82
00:06:01.079 --> 00:06:05.559
IPC keys solve this by giving each operation a unique token.

83
00:06:05.559 --> 00:06:12.239
The sub-agent checks whether it has already completed an operation with that token before executing.

84
00:06:12.239 --> 00:06:16.480
If yes, it returns the previous result without re-executing.

85
00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:21.519
The coordinator passes the same token on retry, and the sub-agent handles the rest.

86
00:06:21.519 --> 00:06:24.739
This has to be designed in from the start.

87
00:06:24.739 --> 00:06:28.339
The final piece of the propagation contract is observability.

88
00:06:28.339 --> 00:06:32.339
The ability to trace an error from a leaf agent all the way through the coordinator

89
00:06:32.339 --> 00:06:34.579
to the user-visible result.

90
00:06:34.579 --> 00:06:39.339
In a multi-agent system with several layers of nesting, a failure at a leaf can travel

91
00:06:39.339 --> 00:06:42.959
through multiple coordinators before it surfaces.

92
00:06:42.959 --> 00:06:47.500
Without deliberate instrumentation, debugging that path is nearly impossible.

93
00:06:47.500 --> 00:06:50.000
The solution is span-based tracing.

94
00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:55.899
Each agent adds its own span identifier to the error envelope before propagating.

95
00:06:55.940 --> 00:07:01.459
The coordinator links these spans into a distributed trace that covers the full call chain.

96
00:07:01.459 --> 00:07:05.820
When an operator investigates a failure, they can follow a single request through every

97
00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:09.899
agent hop and see exactly where it broke and why.

98
00:07:09.899 --> 00:07:14.940
Without this, debugging a propagated error means guessing at the source.

99
00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:20.660
A propagation contract turns the boundary into an interface instead of an accident.

100
00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:25.420
The point is that the answers get decided while the system is calm.

101
00:07:25.420 --> 00:07:27.179
Who owns the recovery?

102
00:07:27.179 --> 00:07:29.579
What a retry is safe to repeat.

103
00:07:29.579 --> 00:07:34.779
Settle that in advance and you're not writing policy while an outage is running.

104
00:07:34.779 --> 00:07:35.899
That does it for this one.

105
00:07:35.899 --> 00:07:37.179
I'll see you in the next lesson.

